# Node.js 26 Goes LTS: Stable TypeScript and What Breaks

 20 hours ago 90 views 0 

Node.js 26 enters Active LTS on October 28, 2026

Node.js 26 enters Active LTS on October 28, 2026. That date matters because it turns stable TypeScript type stripping — running `.ts` files directly without a build step — into something you can actually bet a production system on. The experimental flag is gone. The Temporal API is on by default. But if your codebase uses TypeScript enums, you have a decision to make before you upgrade.

## TypeScript Without a Build Step Is Now LTS

Node.js has run `.ts` files natively since version 22.6 via type stripping: it erases type annotations and runs the JavaScript that’s left. The tool doing that work is amaro, a thin wrapper around the SWC transform engine. Your stack traces keep accurate line numbers. No tsc. No bundler. No config.

What LTS status actually changes is the commitment. The `--experimental-strip-types` flag is gone from Node 26. This is stable API territory now, not a preview you test on Fridays.

The catch — and it matters — is that type stripping is erasure only. Node does not compile TypeScript. Four constructs that generate runtime JavaScript simply do not work:

- Enums — `enum Direction { Up, Down }` compiles to a JavaScript object with reverse mappings; you can’t just erase it
- Namespaces with runtime values
- Constructor parameter properties — the `constructor(private name: string)` shorthand
- Import aliases — `import Alias = Some.Namespace`

Node 26 also removed `--experimental-transform-types`, the escape hatch that previously handled these cases. It is gone with no direct replacement. Your options are to precompile with `tsc`, use a loader like tsx, or refactor to erasable syntax.

For enums specifically, the migration is straightforward:

```typescript
// Before
enum Status {
  Draft = "draft",
  Published = "published",
}

// After (works with Node 26 type stripping)
const Status = {
  Draft: "draft",
  Published: "published",
} as const;
type Status = (typeof Status)[keyof typeof Status];
```

One more thing worth knowing: Node ignores `tsconfig.json` entirely. Path aliases, target downleveling, custom compiler options — none of them apply. If your project relies on `paths` mappings, type stripping alone will not get you there.

## Temporal Is the New Date Object

Node 26 ships Temporal enabled by default — no flag, no polyfill, just there. It is the TC39 Stage 4 proposal that finally replaces the `Date` object, and V8 14.6 includes it natively.

The `Date` object is notoriously broken in ways that are easy to forget until they bite you in production: months are zero-indexed, arithmetic wraps incorrectly across month boundaries (`Jan 31 + 1 month = March 3`), setters mutate in place, and recurring jobs drift across daylight saving transitions. Temporal fixes all of this.

The new types — `PlainDate`, `ZonedDateTime`, `Instant`, `Duration` — are immutable and each model a distinct kind of time:

```javascript
// Today's date
const today = Temporal.Now.plainDateISO();

// Add a month correctly (respects Feb, leap years, etc.)
const nextBilling = today.add({ months: 1 });

// Explicit timezone — no DST drift
const meeting = Temporal.ZonedDateTime.from({
  year: 2026, month: 10, day: 28,
  hour: 9, minute: 0,
  timeZone: 'America/Los_Angeles',
});
```

`Date` is not removed. Existing code continues running. Temporal is the path forward for new code where correctness under DST and calendar edge cases actually matters.

